\section{Caveat for hierarchical models}{

The influences use the \emph{conditional} per-observation log-likelihood (given the
random effects), whereas the IJ of Ji, Lee & Rabe-Hesketh (2024) is derived
for a \emph{marginal} model. For coefficients identified by \emph{within}-cluster
variation (e.g. slopes) the conditional IJ agrees well with the Yang-Wang-He
sandwich. For coefficients identified by \emph{between}-cluster variation (the
intercept of a random-intercept model) it \strong{can under-estimate} the variance
— up-weighting a cluster mostly shifts that cluster's random effect rather
than the fixed effect — and it is noisier; how much depends on the
random-effect-to-noise ratio. For valid fixed-effect inference in the
hierarchical model fitted by \code{bqmm}, prefer the default Yang-Wang-He sandwich
(\code{\link[=ywh_adjust]{ywh_adjust()}}); \code{method = "ij"} is provided for benchmarking.
